The owner's goal, 2026-09-24: pidly packaged so that when publishing is
approved, it can go to every package manager without further work. Hosting
itself is not approved yet, so 53 (tap) and 54 (AUR) are deferred until it is.

Already done: the MIT licence (30), VERSION and --version (52), docs/pidly.1,
the curl install and the release steps in the README. What is missing is the
recipes themselves:

  - packaging/homebrew/pidly.rb — the formula 53 would publish: desc,
    homepage, url of the v<VERSION> tag tarball, sha256, license "MIT",
    bin.install "bin/pidly", man1.install "docs/pidly.1", and a test block
    that runs pidly --version.
  - packaging/aur/PKGBUILD and .SRCINFO — the same for 54: pkgver, source,
    sha256sums, install -Dm755 for the script and -Dm644 for the man page
    and the licence.

The checksum cannot be real until a tag is pushed, which is also publishing.
So the recipes carry the version and a placeholder sha256, and step 4 of the
README's release steps says where the checksum goes.

Something in the suite should fail if a recipe names a version other than
the one in bin/pidly, so a release cannot go out with stale recipes.

Nothing here may make pidly depend on being installed: packaging is
convenience, never capability, as 29 says.

Done when both recipes are in the repo and name the current VERSION, the
suite checks they agree with it, and the README's release steps cover
updating them. Lint them with brew audit and namcap if either is ever to
hand, and say in this ticket if they were not.


Done 2026-09-24. packaging/homebrew/pidly.rb, packaging/aur/PKGBUILD and
packaging/aur/.SRCINFO name 0.1.0 and carry sha256 UNRELEASED, which brew
and makepkg both refuse, until a tag is pushed. The formula's test block runs
pidly --version and pidly init, which works outside a git repo. The
PKGBUILD lists coreutils, findutils, gawk, grep and sed; git is optional,
because it is only used to date tickets that init adopts.

tests/packaging.sh (in run.sh) holds all three to VERSION, the SPDX
licence and each other's checksum and description, and checks the files
they install exist. Checked that it fails on a bumped VERSION and a
mismatched checksum. The README's release steps say how to update them.

NOT linted: this machine has no brew, ruby, makepkg or namcap. Run brew
audit --strict and namcap on them before 53 and 54 publish, and regenerate
.SRCINFO with makepkg --printsrcinfo, since it was written by hand.
